\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2}{\ignorespaces Reliability summaries for secretary-style split-search methods. Top row: empirical probability of achieving a predictive loss below a tolerance \(\varepsilon \). Bottom panels: empirical probability of simultaneously achieving at least a prescribed time saving and a predictive loss below a prescribed tolerance. Separate panels are shown for regression, classification with Gini impurity, and classification with entropy impurity. Size-stratified and method-specific variants are reported in the supplementary material.}}{10}{figure.2}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:reliability}{{2}{10}{Reliability summaries for secretary-style split-search methods. Top row: empirical probability of achieving a predictive loss below a tolerance \(\varepsilon \). Bottom panels: empirical probability of simultaneously achieving at least a prescribed time saving and a predictive loss below a prescribed tolerance. Separate panels are shown for regression, classification with Gini impurity, and classification with entropy impurity. Size-stratified and method-specific variants are reported in the supplementary material}{figure.2}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3}{\ignorespaces Regime maps as a function of dataset size and dimensionality. Each point is a benchmark dataset represented in the \((\log _{10} n,\log _{10} p)\) plane and colored by the split-search strategy that gives the best practical trade-off under a fixed speedup--loss criterion. Columns correspond to loss tolerances \(\tau =5\%,10\%,20\%\). Rows correspond to regression, classification with Gini impurity, and classification with entropy impurity.}}{11}{figure.3}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:dataset_structure}{{3}{11}{Regime maps as a function of dataset size and dimensionality. Each point is a benchmark dataset represented in the \((\log _{10} n,\log _{10} p)\) plane and colored by the split-search strategy that gives the best practical trade-off under a fixed speedup--loss criterion. Columns correspond to loss tolerances \(\tau =5\%,10\%,20\%\). Rows correspond to regression, classification with Gini impurity, and classification with entropy impurity}{figure.3}{}}
